## Findings

### #1 [CRITICAL] Hardcoded credentials / secrets (AP-01)
File: app.py:13
File: services/notification_service.py:7-10
Description: `app.config['SECRET_KEY'] = 'super-secret-key-123'` is a literal in the entry point. `NotificationService.__init__` hardcodes the SMTP host, the user `'taskmanager@gmail.com'` and `self.email_password = 'senha123'`.
Impact: Anyone with access to the repo or its git history gets the Flask signing key and the mailbox credentials. Neither can be rotated per environment without editing code.
Recommendation: Move SECRET_KEY, the DB URI and the SMTP settings into a config module that reads environment variables, and ship a `.env.example` without real values (Playbook P-01).

### #2 [CRITICAL] God Method / God File (AP-03)
File: routes/report_routes.py:12-101
File: routes/report_routes.py:157-223
File: routes/task_routes.py:11-63
Description: `summary_report` is 90 lines. It runs 12 separate queries, computes overdue lists, 7-day activity and per-user productivity, and builds the response, all in one function. `report_routes.py` also holds the full Category CRUD (`/categories`), which is a second domain inside the reports blueprint. `get_tasks` is 53 lines and mixes querying, overdue rules, joins done by hand and serialization. Each of these files carries routing, HTTP parsing, validation, business logic, data access and serialization (6 responsibilities).
Impact: None of this can be unit-tested without an HTTP request context, and any change to reports or categories touches the same file.
Recommendation: Split these into `CategoryController`, `ReportController` and `TaskController`, with category routes in their own blueprint. Route handlers should only map HTTP to a controller (Playbook P-03 + P-04).

### #3 [CRITICAL] Destructive/privileged endpoints without authentication (AP-04)
File: routes/user_routes.py:134-151
File: routes/user_routes.py:119-125
File: routes/user_routes.py:52
File: routes/user_routes.py:71
Description: `DELETE /users/<id>` deletes any user and all of their tasks (`for t in tasks: db.session.delete(t)`) with no identity check. `POST /users` accepts `role` from the body (`data.get('role', 'user')`, allowing `'admin'`). `PUT /users/<id>` lets any caller set `role` and `active` on any account.
Impact: Any anonymous client can make itself admin, deactivate other users or wipe a user's data.
Recommendation: Protect user deletion and any assignment of a privileged role or `active` status with an admin-token middleware (`X-Admin-Token`, constant-time comparison, fail-closed) (Playbook P-12).

### #4 [CRITICAL] Insecure password hashing (AP-05)
File: models/user.py:27-29
File: models/user.py:31-32
Description: `self.password = hashlib.md5(pwd.encode()).hexdigest()` hashes passwords with unsalted MD5. `check_password` compares with `==`, which is not constant time.
Impact: A leaked database lets an attacker recover passwords instantly with rainbow tables. The seed passwords `1234`, `abcd` and `pass` would be cracked trivially.
Recommendation: Use `werkzeug.security.generate_password_hash` and `check_password_hash` (already installed with Flask). Keep verifying existing MD5 hashes during a transition (Playbook P-05).

### #5 [CRITICAL] Sensitive data exposure — password hash in responses (AP-06)
File: models/user.py:21
File: routes/user_routes.py:33
File: routes/user_routes.py:85
File: routes/user_routes.py:129
File: routes/user_routes.py:209
Description: `User.to_dict()` includes `'password': self.password`. That dict is returned by `GET /users/<id>`, `POST /users`, `PUT /users/<id>` and `POST /login`.
Impact: Every client can read the MD5 hash of any user, and combined with #4 that gives them the plaintext password.
Recommendation: Use an explicit user serializer that never includes `password` (Playbook P-06).

### #6 [HIGH] Business logic in route handlers — Fat Routes (AP-07)
File: routes/task_routes.py:85-154
File: routes/task_routes.py:156-223
File: routes/task_routes.py:273-299
File: routes/user_routes.py:42-90
File: routes/user_routes.py:92-132
File: routes/report_routes.py:103-155
Description: The handlers call `Task.query`, `User.query` and `db.session` directly. They also do the validation (title length, status, priority), check that referenced user/category exist, parse dates, join tags, and compute statistics and completion rates inline.
Impact: The business rules are tied to Flask and cannot be reused or tested on their own. The same rules drift between create and update.
Recommendation: Move the flow into controllers that receive their dependencies (models/session) by injection, and keep each route to under ~10 lines (Playbook P-04).

### #7 [HIGH] Tight coupling without dependency injection (AP-08)
File: services/notification_service.py:5-10
File: services/notification_service.py:15
File: routes/task_routes.py:2
File: routes/user_routes.py:2
File: routes/report_routes.py:2
Description: `NotificationService` builds its own config and opens `smtplib.SMTP(...)` directly inside `send_email`. Every route module imports the global `db` from `database` and the concrete models, with no seam for substitution.
Impact: Tests cannot swap in a fake mailer or session, and changing infrastructure means editing the business code.
Recommendation: Use a composition root (app factory) that builds the config, the session and the controllers. Inject the SMTP settings and the sender into the notification service (Playbook P-07).

### #8 [HIGH] Insecure runtime config / fake authentication (AP-12)
File: app.py:34
File: routes/user_routes.py:210
File: app.py:15
Description: `app.run(debug=True, host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)` exposes the Werkzeug debugger on every interface. Login returns `'token': 'fake-jwt-token-' + str(user.id)`, a predictable unsigned token. `CORS(app)` allows every origin.
Impact: The debugger console allows remote code execution. Anyone can forge a "token" for any user id.
Recommendation: Read DEBUG, HOST, PORT and CORS origins from the environment with safe defaults. Issue a signed token (`itsdangerous`, which ships with Flask, keyed with SECRET_KEY) and keep the response shape (Playbook P-01, P-12).

### #9 [MEDIUM] Mutable in-memory state on a service instance (AP-09)
File: services/notification_service.py:6
File: services/notification_service.py:31-36
Description: `self.notifications = []` keeps notification history in process memory and grows without bound. This is downgraded from HIGH because `NotificationService` is never instantiated anywhere in the app, so the impact is currently limited.
Impact: If the service were wired in, history would be lost on restart, inconsistent across workers and leak memory.
Recommendation: Remove the in-memory store (or inject a store) and keep the service stateless (Playbook P-07 / P-08).

### #10 [MEDIUM] N+1 queries / repeated counts (AP-13)
File: routes/task_routes.py:41-57
File: routes/user_routes.py:22
File: routes/report_routes.py:53-68
File: routes/report_routes.py:163
File: routes/report_routes.py:15-28
File: routes/task_routes.py:275-281
Description: `get_tasks` calls `User.query.get` and `Category.query.get` for every task. `get_users` triggers a lazy load with `len(u.tasks)` per user. `summary_report` runs `Task.query.filter_by(user_id=u.id).all()` per user. `get_categories` runs a `count()` per category. Status and priority counts are 9 separate `COUNT` queries plus a full `Task.query.all()`, and the same pattern appears in `task_stats`.
Impact: Latency grows linearly with the number of tasks, users and categories.
Recommendation: Use eager loading (`joinedload`/`selectinload`) and `GROUP BY` aggregations (Playbook P-13).

### #11 [MEDIUM] Missing/inconsistent input validation (AP-14)
File: routes/task_routes.py:113
File: routes/task_routes.py:166-170
File: routes/task_routes.py:182
File: routes/task_routes.py:260-264
File: routes/report_routes.py:196-197
File: routes/user_routes.py:102-103
File: routes/report_routes.py:180
Description: `priority < 1` with a string priority raises `TypeError`, which becomes a 500. `len(data['title'])` with `title: null` raises a 500. `int(priority)` and `int(user_id)` in search raise `ValueError` on non-numeric query params. `update_category` uses `'name' in data` without a `None` check, so an empty body gives a 500. `update_user` accepts an empty `name`. Category `color` is not validated even though `is_valid_color` exists. `process_task_data` and `validate_email` in `utils/helpers.py` are unused and reimplemented inline.
Impact: Malformed input produces 500s instead of 400s, and create and update enforce different rules.
Recommendation: Use one validator per resource, shared by create and update, that raises a 400 domain error (Playbook P-14).

### #12 [MEDIUM] Duplicated code (AP-15)
File: routes/task_routes.py:30-39
File: routes/task_routes.py:71-80
File: routes/task_routes.py:283-287
File: routes/user_routes.py:171-180
File: routes/report_routes.py:33-37
File: routes/report_routes.py:132-135
File: routes/task_routes.py:17-28
File: routes/user_routes.py:162-169
Description: The "overdue" rule is reimplemented 6 times even though `Task.is_overdue()` exists (models/task.py:50-60). Task serialization is hand-built in `get_tasks` and `get_user_tasks` instead of using `Task.to_dict()`. Title, status and priority checks are duplicated between create and update (task_routes.py:96-114 vs 166-184). The email regex is duplicated (user_routes.py:61, 106), and completion rate is computed in 3 places.
Impact: When a rule changes, it has to be edited in many places, which risks inconsistent behavior.
Recommendation: Keep each rule in one place in the model or domain and use one serializer per entity (Playbook P-06, P-15).

### #13 [MEDIUM] Non-centralized error handling (AP-16)
File: routes/task_routes.py:62
File: routes/task_routes.py:137
File: routes/task_routes.py:151-154
File: routes/task_routes.py:204
File: routes/user_routes.py:87-90
File: routes/report_routes.py:186
File: utils/helpers.py:46-50
File: services/notification_service.py:23-25
Description: There are 16 `except:` / `except Exception` blocks (+8 occurrences beyond those listed), each building its own error response, and there is no `@app.errorhandler`. `get_tasks` wraps the whole handler in a bare `except:` that hides every failure. `str(e)` is printed to stdout.
Impact: Bugs are swallowed silently, and error handling is inconsistent and impossible to audit.
Recommendation: Add domain exceptions (`NotFound`, `ValidationError`, `Conflict`, `Forbidden`) and a central error handler that keeps the `{'error': msg}` format (Playbook P-11).

### #14 [MEDIUM] Deprecated APIs (AP-17)
File: routes/task_routes.py:42
File: routes/task_routes.py:51
File: routes/user_routes.py:29
File: routes/report_routes.py:105
File: routes/report_routes.py:192
File: models/task.py:15-16
File: models/user.py:14
File: models/category.py:11
Description: `Model.query.get(id)` appears 18 times across the 3 route files. It is the legacy SQLAlchemy 2.x API (Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.1.1) and emits `LegacyAPIWarning`. `datetime.utcnow()` and `default=datetime.utcnow` appear in models, routes, services and helpers (+20 occurrences); this is deprecated since Python 3.12.
Impact: Warnings today and breakage when the legacy APIs are removed or Python is upgraded.
Recommendation: Use `db.session.get(Model, id)` and a `utcnow()` helper based on `datetime.now(timezone.utc).replace(tzinfo=None)` (the columns are naive).

